{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"338c2f39-725d-4f66-8093-72e64c4dd02f","title":"Doppler effect with circular wavefronts"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"338c2f39-725d-4f66-8093-72e64c4dd02f","step":{"id":"s1","script":"A source moves right while emitting sound at equal time intervals. Each circular wavefront expands from where the source was when emitted.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Moving source, still air"},{"op":"figure","id":"waves","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-4.8,3],"y":[-3.4,3.4],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"circle","id":"oldwave","center":[-1.2,0],"r":3},{"kind":"circle","id":"midwave","center":[-0.8,0],"r":2},{"kind":"point","id":"oldpos","at":[-1.2,0]},{"kind":"point","id":"midpos","at":[-0.8,0]},{"kind":"point","id":"source","at":[0,0],"label":"source"},{"kind":"segment","from":[0,0.35],"to":[0.9,0.35],"arrow":true,"label":"vₛ"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"338c2f39-725d-4f66-8093-72e64c4dd02f","step":{"id":"s2","script":"The newest wave is smaller and centered farther right. The source approaches its forward wavefronts, crowding them together.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"newwave","on":"waves","marks":[{"kind":"circle","id":"youngwave","center":[-0.4,0],"r":1},{"kind":"point","id":"youngpos","at":[-0.4,0]},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"oldpos"},"to":{"at":"source"},"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"text","at":[1.9,2.5],"text":"ahead"},{"kind":"text","at":[-3.5,2.5],"text":"behind"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"period","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"T=\\frac{1}{f_s},\\qquad v_s<c"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"338c2f39-725d-4f66-8093-72e64c4dd02f","step":{"id":"s3","script":"In one emission period, sound travels c T while the source moves v sub s T. Subtract ahead; add behind.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"spacing","on":"waves","marks":[{"kind":"measure","from":[0.6,0],"to":[1.2,0],"label":"λ ahead"},{"kind":"measure","from":[-2.8,0],"to":[-1.4,0],"label":"λ behind"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"wavelengths","anchor":{"el":"period","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"\\lambda_{\\text{ahead}}=(c-v_s)T\\qquad\\lambda_{\\text{behind}}=(c+v_s)T"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"For a stationary listener ahead, is the heard frequency higher or lower than the source frequency?","expected_gist":"Higher: the wavefronts are closer together."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"338c2f39-725d-4f66-8093-72e64c4dd02f","step":{"id":"s4","script":"A stationary listener hears frequency c divided by wavelength. Shorter spacing ahead means higher pitch; wider spacing behind means lower pitch.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"pitch","on":"waves","marks":[{"kind":"text","at":[1.65,-1.8],"text":"higher pitch","size":"small"},{"kind":"text","at":[-3.1,-2.5],"text":"lower pitch","size":"small"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"frequencies","anchor":{"el":"wavelengths","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"f_{\\text{ahead}}=f_s\\frac{c}{c-v_s}\\qquad f_{\\text{behind}}=f_s\\frac{c}{c+v_s}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
